## Exam-Style Practice

The following paper-friendly example is independent of the bike-rental analysis. A food-processing plant records one observation per operating day. The outcome is daily electricity use in MWh; the predictor is scheduled production hours. Across observed days, production hours range from 8 to 24.

The fitted simple regression is:

\[
electricity use = 18.00 + 0.75(production\ hours).
\]

| Term | Estimate | Standard error | 95% confidence interval | p-value |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Intercept | 18.00 | 3.00 | [12.10, 23.90] | <0.001 |
| Production hours | 0.75 | 0.20 | [0.35, 1.15] | <0.001 |

On one 20-hour day, actual electricity use was 35 MWh. The sample mean was 30 MWh.

### Block A — Coefficients, fitted value, and residual

<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-red">RED</span> Without R or AI: (a) interpret the slope with units and associational language; (b) interpret the intercept and explain whether it is interpolation or extrapolation; (c) calculate the fitted value for the 20-hour day; and (d) calculate and interpret its residual.</p>

### Block B — Uncertainty and claim audit

<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-red">RED</span> Without R or AI: (a) interpret the slope standard error; (b) interpret the 95% confidence interval; (c) state the null tested by the slope p-value and what the reported evidence means; and (d) explain why the output does not establish that adding one scheduled production hour causes electricity use to rise by exactly 0.75 MWh.</p>

### Block C — Statistical versus practical importance

